PART 4: HOW V3 VEIL GLASS WILL BE IMPLEMENTED ACROSS THE APP
Per the authoritative V3_Veil_Glass_Design_Specs.md and V3_Implementation_Specs.md:

The Core Principle: Single-Glass Illusion
The entire application must feel like one continuous premium matte glass surface floating over a deep slate-navy canvas. There is no "page redesign" in the traditional sense — instead, every view is a content state within the same glass card. When the user navigates from Dashboard → Evidence → Policy → Calendar, the glass card stays still and the content inside it dissolves, slides, or cross-fades.

Implementation Phases (from V3_Implementation_Specs.md)
Phase 1: Foundation

Add V3 tokens as CSS custom properties (--ci-veil-*)
Create glassMode flag in shell store ('v3-veil' | 'current')
Implement global .no-scrollbar and .v3-invisible-glare utilities
Phase 2: Shell Evolution

Update ShellNavRail and CommandCenterLayout to support:
Transparent sidebar (no glass treatment on drawer)
Collapsible hierarchical submenus with nested left borders
Single interrupted vertical divider (rgba(255,255,255,0.12))
Modify ShellContentFrame to render the 77.7% constrained glass card (min 980px, 92vh, centered)
Right-side cast shadow: 30px 10px 80px rgba(0,0,0,0.9)
No outer border on main card
Phase 3: Core Surfaces

Convert all surfaces to the V3 invisible vs. bordered philosophy:
Invisible (default): KPIs, banners, most planner cards — transparent bg, no border, v3-invisible-glare hover
Bordered: Task cards, Kanban items, data table rows, interactive elements — rgba(255,255,255,0.33) border
Interactive Glass: Buttons, active states — 0.33 → 0.45 opacity on hover
Eliminate all parallel design systems: CES navy/orange, eCign navy/orange, Journey cinematic dark — all converge to V3 teal dominance
Color enforcement: Teal for everything (#007970 / #00D1C1). Orange (#E07B2C / #FFA059) used exclusively for the "Command Center" eyebrow glow and "My Personal Workspace" label. No other orange usage permitted.
